    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Myb (Myeloblastosis) family of proteins in humans is responsible for many forms of cancer and is implicated in a cell cycle pathway.  A group of five proteins called MuvB (Multivulval Class B) interacts with Myb to form the active Myb-MuvB-Core complex.  The MuvB core complex also interacts with three other proteins to form the regulatory DREAM (DP, RBF, E2F2, and Myb-interacting proteins) complex.  Using Drosophila melanogaster, we analyzed a mutant of Mip120 (Myb-interacting protein 120), one member of the MuvB core complex for its ability to associate with the other members of the complex. We created a Mip120 mutant because we believe a region of Mip120 that we identified and deleted may contain a serine-threonine phosphorylation site and be involved in associating with other members of the complex.  Mip120 can bind to DNA and associate with Myb and RBF (Retinoblastoma-family) but it is unclear how Mip120 associates with other members of the complexes.  A Mip120 mutant with a deletion in the identified region (Mip120-del136-145) was created and overexpressed in vivo to compare phenotypes and viability with wild-type Mip120 overexpression.  Co-immunoprecipitation was used to determine the ability of Mip120-del136-145 to associate to other members of the complexes.  The region deleted from Mip120-del136-145 was shown to have altered lethality when overexpressed in vivo but does not affect association to complex members p55 Caf1, Mip130, and Mip40 in the presence of endogenous Mip120 in vitro. These results opens new avenues of investigation into how the Myb-MuvB-Core and DREAM complexes regulate cell proliferation and differentiation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
